{# Shared chrome. Every content template wraps its body in `stage(s)`, where `s` is the
   slide-model slide — so the macros read section/title/highlights/reveal/lead off it directly. #}
{# The head block: section pill, title, and the `lead` — which is the title's own sub-line, not
   the body's first paragraph. It lives here rather than inside `.cfit` for the same reason a
   `source` lives outside it: the fit pass scales and repositions `.cfit`, so a lead emitted there
   drifted down and shrank with the body instead of staying with the title it introduces. Emitting
   it once here also means no template has to remember to call it. #}
{% macro header(s) %}
{% if s.section %}<span class="pill">{{ s.section }}</span>{% endif %}
{% if s.title %}<h2 class="stitle">{{ s.title }}</h2>{% endif %}
{% if s.lead %}<p class="lead">{{ s.lead }}</p>{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}
{# One accented band of highlights. Called up to three times by `stage()` — above the body for
   the entries marked `position: top`, below it for the rest, and once more for the `source`
   entries — so every band is literally the same piece in a different place: same classes, same
   per-`kind` colour and icon. #}
{% macro hlband(items, extra, f) %}<div class="highlights{{ extra }}{{ f }}">{% for h in items %}<div class="highlight {{ h.kind }}">{% if h.icon %}{{ icon(h.icon) }}{% endif %}<p>{% if h.label %}<b>{{ h.label | nocolon }}</b>: {% endif %}{{ h.body }}</p></div>{% endfor %}</div>{% endmacro %}
{# The stage owns the slide's **design** — how the canvas is divided — and the template owns only
   its **style**, the shape the content takes. `s.design` (resolved in html_style.py `_design`,
   which maps the old `layout`/`image`/`aside` spellings forward) places `s.media`:

     full          content uses the whole stage — no media placed
     banded        media across the top, content in a band below it
     split-left    media and content share the canvas, media on the left
     split-right   …on the right
     column-left   a narrow full-bleed column of media down the left edge, cropped to fill
     column-right  …down the right edge
     bleed         media fills the stage; the content sits over it

   Markup order is always head → content → media, in every design: the reading order (and the PDF
   export and screen-reader order) must not depend on which side the picture landed on, so the
   mirroring is done entirely in CSS.

   Highlights split by `position`: the **top** band frames what follows, so it is on screen from
   the moment the slide opens (no `frag`) — a frame that arrives after the body frames nothing.
   The **bottom** band comments on what precedes it, so it keeps its place as the final fragment. #}
{% macro stage(s) %}
{# `source` leaves the flow entirely. The other kinds are remarks *on* the body, so they belong
   inside `.cfit` where they move and scale with it; a citation is slide chrome — it credits the
   material rather than saying anything about it — so it is pinned to the bottom edge of the stage
   as a sibling of `.cbody`. Being outside `.cfit` is the whole point: it neither drifts up with
   short content nor gets shrunk by the fit pass. `position` does not apply to it. #}
{% set hl = s.highlights or [] %}
{% set hlsrc = hl | selectattr('kind', 'eq', 'source') | list %}
{% set hlrest = hl | rejectattr('kind', 'eq', 'source') | list %}
{% set hltop = hlrest | selectattr('position', 'eq', 'top') | list %}
{% set hlbot = hlrest | rejectattr('position', 'eq', 'top') | list %}
{# `split-left` is two independent facts — the shape (a split) and the side the media takes — so
   they become two classes. Written as one, every split and column rule had to be spelled twice,
   once per side; written as two, `.d-split` carries what both sides share and only the placement
   mentions `.side-left`. #}
{% set p = s.design.split('-') %}
<div class="stage d-{{ p[0] }}{{ ' side-' ~ p[1] if p|length > 1 else '' }}">
<div class="shead">{{ header(s) }}</div><div class="cbody"><div class="cfit">{% if hltop %}{{ hlband(hltop, ' hl-top', '') }}{% endif %}{{ caller() }}
{% if hlbot %}{{ hlband(hlbot, '', frag(s)) }}{% endif %}
</div></div>
{% if hlsrc %}{{ hlband(hlsrc, ' hl-source', '') }}{% endif %}
{# guarded on the *design*, not on `s.media`: a slide can carry media the resolved design does
   not place (a legacy `image` on a template that never composed one), and emitting it anyway
   would drop an unplaced picture into a stage with no rule to position it. #}
{% if s.design != 'full' %}<div class="smedia">{{ embed_img(s.media) }}</div>{% endif %}
</div>
{% endmacro %}
{# Enumeration items appear one at a time (Reveal fragments) by default; `reveal: together`
   opts out and shows them all at once. Negated on purpose: an unrecognized value animates,
   which is the default anyone wants — the opt-in form failed silently on every typo.
   Every item animates in, the first one included — the slide opens on its heading alone and
   the presenter clicks in each item. Group containers (e.g. highlights) call `frag(s)` too and
   stay a single fragment. #}
{% macro frag(s) %}{{ '' if s.reveal == 'together' else ' fragment fade-up' }}{% endmacro %}
